# Heldar dashboard — TLS termination via Caddy.
#
# TWO modes, selected purely by env (see deploy/TLS.md):
#   Public domain + Let's Encrypt : HELDAR_TLS_DOMAIN=cam.example.com   HELDAR_TLS_ISSUER=you@example.com
#   LAN self-signed (default)     : HELDAR_TLS_DOMAIN=https://192.168.1.50  HELDAR_TLS_ISSUER=internal
#
# Port model: Caddy binds :80 and :443 on the host (host networking) and reverse-proxies CLEARTEXT
# to the existing nginx dashboard on 127.0.0.1:8080, which in turn proxies /api,/media,/healthz to
# the kernel on 127.0.0.1:8000. :80 exists for the ACME HTTP-01 challenge and the automatic
# HTTP->HTTPS redirect; all real traffic is :443.
#
# The BROWSER media plane (HLS + WHEP) is proxied here too. It has to be: MediaMTX serves those on
# plaintext 8888/8889, and an `http://host:8888/…` URL on an `https://` page is blocked as mixed
# content, so live view would be dead over TLS. The kernel emits origin-relative `/live/hls` and
# `/live/whep` URLs when HELDAR_MEDIA_SAME_ORIGIN=true (set by compose.tls.yml) and this file maps
# them back onto MediaMTX. RTSP is NOT proxied — it is not a browser protocol and cannot ride the
# HTTPS origin; the WebRTC remote-access path also keeps its own separate transport.
{
	# ACME account / internal-CA root are persisted on the caddy-data volume (see compose.tls.yml)
	# so certs and the self-signed root survive restarts. Admin API off — nothing should reach it
	# on a host-networked box.
	admin off
}

# Shared dashboard security headers. HSTS is meaningful for public domains; browsers ignore HSTS
# sent for bare IP addresses, so it is harmless in LAN self-signed mode.
(heldar_security_headers) {
	header {
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		X-Frame-Options "DENY"
		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
		# Don't advertise the reverse proxy.
		-Server
	}
}

{$HELDAR_TLS_DOMAIN:localhost} {
	# `internal` -> Caddy's local CA (LAN self-signed). An email address -> ACME / Let's Encrypt
	# for the public domain above.
	tls {$HELDAR_TLS_ISSUER:internal}

	import heldar_security_headers

	# --- Browser media plane -------------------------------------------------------------------
	# These two prefixes are a contract with the kernel (MEDIA_HLS_PREFIX / MEDIA_WHEP_PREFIX in
	# crates/heldar-kernel/src/services/mediamtx.rs). Strip the prefix and hand the rest to MediaMTX
	# on loopback, so the browser only ever talks HTTPS to this origin.
	#
	# The read token rides as a query string and is preserved by the rewrite. MediaMTX HLS playlists
	# reference their segments RELATIVELY, so segment fetches resolve back under /live/hls/... and are
	# proxied by the same rule.
	#
	# The Location rewrite is REQUIRED, not defensive. `handle_path` strips the prefix, so MediaMTX
	# believes it is mounted at the origin root and its redirects say so: an HLS request is answered
	# with `302 Location: /<path>/index.m3u8?cookieCheck=1`. Without re-adding the prefix the browser
	# follows that to the dashboard's SPA fallback, gets index.html with a 200, and the player fails
	# on HTML it cannot parse — live view silently broken in a way that looks like a camera fault.
	# `^/` only matches a path-absolute Location, so an absolute URL is left alone.
	handle_path /live/hls/* {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8888 {
			header_down Location ^/ /live/hls/
		}
	}
	# WHEP is an HTTP POST (SDP offer -> answer) plus PATCH for ICE trickle; a plain reverse_proxy
	# carries all of it. Media itself flows over WebRTC/UDP directly, NOT through Caddy. Same Location
	# rewrite, for the same reason.
	handle_path /live/whep/* {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8889 {
			header_down Location ^/ /live/whep/
		}
	}

	# --- Dashboard + API -----------------------------------------------------------------------
	# Single cleartext hop to the existing dashboard container (nginx on :8080), loopback only.
	# Caddy adds X-Forwarded-Proto/-For/Host automatically; the kernel marks the session cookie
	# Secure from HELDAR_AUTH_COOKIE_SECURE (compose.prod.yml), independent of this hop's scheme.
	handle {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080
	}
}
